# Reviewed gitleaks false positives, one fingerprint per line (commit:file:rule:line).
# Read by the "Secrets (gitleaks)" job in .github/workflows/security.yml via --gitleaks-ignore-path.
#
# A fingerprint names ONE finding in ONE commit. The same value committed again, in another
# file or on another line, is a new finding and fails again: that is the point. Never add a
# path-wide allowlist to gitleaks.toml instead.
#
# To add one: run the scan (BCWEB/guides/run/SECURITY_CI_EN.md, "Gitleaks"), read the finding
# in the redacted report, confirm the value is not a credential, and add its Fingerprint below
# with a comment saying WHY, the reviewer and the date. A real secret is never listed here: it
# is rotated first, and only then may its fingerprint be listed, with "rotated on <date>".
#
# Reviewed 2026-09-25 (agent-secci-bcw), every value read in the commit it was found in.

# TELEMETRY_API_KEY in BCWEB/infra/compose/.env.example (the telemetry INGEST key). Public by
# design, not a credential: it ships inside every copy of the BMM desktop app
# (frontend/src/core/links-config.ts, analytics_key, commented "PUBLIC ingest key — safe to
# ship") and only lets a client SUBMIT opt-in telemetry; reading, exporting and deleting need
# TELEMETRY_ADMIN_KEY, which is not in any commit. The .env.example itself says "NOT a secret,
# despite the name". Verified 2026-09-25 (agent-findings-bcw) by comparing the committed values
# with the client's, without printing them: the 9abfe0e3 value is the one the current BMM
# client ships; the 63ecc8c6 value is the previous one, which the BMM client source carried
# before (BMM history: commits 6caec539, c508d605, a96065cc touch it). Rotating it means
# shipping a new BMM client, and gains nothing: whoever has the app has the key.
